  summary: `# 🎓 Preclinic Orientation — OSCE ปฐมนิเทศ

## 🎯 OSCE = Objective Structured Clinical Examination
- Practical exam, multiple stations
- Each station: ~5-15 minutes
- Standardized — same scenario for all students
- Assessed by trained examiners with checklist
- Used for licensing + competency verification

## 📋 OSCE structure (typical CUVET)

### Number of stations
- Usually 10-20 stations per exam
- Mix of: physical exam, procedures, communication, interpretation

### Station types

#### Procedural stations
- Demonstrate a skill (e.g., venipuncture, suture, IV catheter)
- Performance assessed live
- Common: physical exam, intubation, catheter, suture, FNA, otoscopy

#### Diagnostic stations
- Interpret radiograph, blood smear, urinalysis
- Identify findings + provide interpretation
- Time-limited

#### Communication stations
- Standardized client (actor)
- Take history, deliver bad news, obtain consent
- Communication skills assessed

#### Knowledge integration stations
- Case scenario presented
- Develop DDx, plan workup, justify
- Often verbal + written

## 🩺 Key OSCE skills to master

### Physical exam (small animal)

#### Checklist (typical 5-min station)
1. Introduce yourself + ask for consent (PPE on)
2. General observation (BCS, mentation, posture)
3. TPR (temp, pulse, resp)
4. Mucous membranes (color, CRT)
5. Lymph nodes (palpation — submandibular, prescapular, axillary, inguinal, popliteal)
6. Eyes, ears, oral exam
7. Auscultate heart (5 PMI points: aortic, pulmonic, tricuspid, mitral, plus apex)
8. Auscultate lungs (3-field at minimum)
9. Abdominal palpation (cranial, mid, caudal)
10. Limb + neurologic screen
11. Skin/coat exam
12. Conclude + thank
13. State findings to examiner

### Venipuncture
- Identify vein (cephalic most common)
- Aseptic prep (alcohol, scrub)
- Hold off vein (apply pressure proximal)
- Insert needle bevel-up at 15-30°
- Aspirate gently (don't collapse vein)
- Release pressure on vessel before withdrawing needle
- Apply pressure for hemostasis
- Label sample correctly

### IV catheter placement
- Same prep as venipuncture
- Use angiocath (insert + advance catheter, withdraw stylet)
- Confirm in vein (flush with saline)
- Secure with tape (chevron pattern)
- Connect IV line, set rate

### Suture
- Common patterns: simple interrupted, simple continuous, intradermal, mattress
- Knot tying: square knot, surgeon's knot
- Tension management
- Use needle holder properly

### Cytology slide prep
- FNA technique (capillary or aspiration)
- Squash prep technique
- Stain (Wright-Giemsa, Diff-Quik)
- Look at slide systematically (low → high power)

### Cardiac auscultation
- Identify PMI (point of maximal intensity)
- Grade murmur (1-6)
- Identify systolic vs diastolic (synchronize with pulse)
- Recognize: split S2, gallop, click

## 💬 Communication stations

### Common scenarios
- New puppy/kitten exam — vaccination + care plan
- Senior pet — geriatric concern (anorexia, weight loss)
- Bad news — terminal diagnosis (cancer, heart failure)
- Behavior consult — aggression
- Euthanasia decision — quality of life
- Cost discussion — treatment options + budget

### Communication framework (Calgary-Cambridge or 4E)
- **E**ngage: build rapport, introduce
- **E**mpathize: acknowledge feelings
- **E**ducate: explain + check understanding
- **E**nlist: collaborative plan

### Tips
- Avoid jargon (use "tummy" not "abdomen")
- Open questions early ("Tell me about your dog")
- Closed questions to confirm
- Pause for questions
- Summarize at end

## 📝 OSCE preparation tips

### Practice stations with peers
- Group practice + feedback
- Time yourself (mimic exam)
- Use checklists to grade each other

### Simulators
- Manikin work (CPR, intubation, catheter)
- Surgical pad practice (suture, knot)

### Clinical exposure
- Practice in real cases during rotations
- Ask attending for feedback
- Watch experienced clinicians

### Knowledge review
- Common conditions per organ system
- Top 100 dx + treatments
- Imaging interpretation patterns
- Clin path normal ranges

## 🎯 OSCE day tips
- Arrive early (lower stress)
- Eat breakfast
- Wear professional attire (white coat, ID, name tag)
- Bring stethoscope (your own)
- Don't dwell on previous station — move on
- Listen to instructions carefully
- Communicate with the examiner (verbalize what you're doing)
- Watch the clock — pacing matters

## 📝 KEY POINTS
1. OSCE = practical, multiple stations, time-limited
2. Physical exam: systematic + checklist-driven
3. Procedural skills: practice repeatedly
4. Communication: empathy + clear language
5. Bring own stethoscope, professional attire`,
